ped inside a banana leaf, commonly found in Indonesia, Malaysia, and Singapore. Rice is rolled inside a banana leaf and boiled, then cut into small cakes as a staple food replacement for steamed rice. The texture is similar to that of ketupat, with the difference being that the ketupat container is made from woven janur (young coconut leaf) fronds, while lontong uses banana leaves instead."]],"src":"wikipedia","actions":[{"kind":"globe","hash":"ingredient-lontong"},{"kind":"wiki","url":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Lontong"}],"sections":[{"h":"Where it's cooked with","count":1,"groups":[["Indonesian cuisine","cuisine/indonesian-cuisine",[["Coto makassar","dish/coto-makassar"],["Gado-gado","dish/gado-gado"],["Ketoprak (dish)","dish/ketoprak-dish"],["Lontong dekem","dish/lontong-dekem"],["Rujak soto","dish/rujak-soto"],["Tahu campur","dish/tahu-campur"]]]]},{"h":"Often cooked with","links":[["Tofu","ingredient/tofu"],["Beef","ingredient/beef"],["Lemongrass","ingredient/lemongrass"],["Palm sugar","ingredient/palm-sugar"],["Krupuk","ingredient/krupuk"],["Caraway","ingredient/caraway"],["Celery","ingredient/celery"],["Chili pepper","ingredient/chili-pepper"],["Coconut milk","ingredient/coconut-milk"],["Galangal","ingredient/galangal"],["Ginger","ingredient/ginger"],["Scallion","ingredient/scallion"],["Cucumber","ingredient/cucumber"],["Peanut sauce","ingredient/peanut-sauce"],["Rice cake","ingredient/rice-cake"],["Mung bean sprout","ingredient/mung-bean-sprout"]]}],"img":{"src":"/world-cuisine/img/thumb/4/4e/Lontong.jpg/500px-Lontong.jpg","w":500,"h":375,"by":"No machine-readable author provided. Diádoco assumed (based on copyright claims).","lic":"CC BY 3.0","licUrl":"https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by/3.0","page":"https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Lontong.jpg"}},"dish/crumble":{"t":"dish","r":"dish/crumble","n":"Crumble","c":"#ede6da","crumb":[["Dishes","dishes"]],"sub":"Dish found in 2 cuisines","dot":"#cf5f87","intro":[["A crumble (British English) or crisp (American English) is a baked fruit dessert with a streusel like topping, made with flour, sugar and fat (normally butter). They were simple dishes until after World War II, when they started to appear with recipes containing the addition of oats, nuts or coconut. With the popular fillings of apple and rhubarb for sweet dessert varieties, there are savoury fillings which contain meat, cheese or vegetables. As a dessert, crumbles and crisps are traditionally served with custard, cream, or ice cream."]],"src":"wikipedia","actions":[{"kind":"globe","hash":"dish-crumble"},{"kind":"wiki","url":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Crumble"}],"sections":[{"h":"Where it's from","links":[["British cuisine","cuisine/british-cuisine"],["Irish cuisine","cuisine/irish-cuisine"]]},{"h":"Ingredients","count":6,"links":[["Butter","ingredient/butter"],["Cheese","ingredient/cheese"],["Streusel","ingredient/streusel"],["Sugar","ingredient/sugar"],["Vegetables","ingredient/vegetables"],["Wheat flour","ingredient/wheat-flour"]]},{"h":"Cook it","rows":[{"name":"Great British Chefs: traditional British recipes","color":"#55b8ad","shape":"site","sub":"greatbritishchefs.com","note":"UK chef-driven recipe site with a large section of classic British dishes.","url":"https://www.greatbritishchefs.com/features/our-most-popular-traditional-british-recipes","suggested":true,"kind":"site"},{"name":"Irish American Mom","color":"#55b8ad","shape":"site","sub":"irishamericanmom.com","note":"Irish-born cook living in the US sharing traditional Irish recipes.","url":"https://www.irishamericanmom.com/recipes/","suggested":true,"kind":"blog"}]},{"h":"More from British cuisine","links":[["Banbury cake","dish/banbury-cake"],["Battenberg cake","dish/battenberg-cake"],["Black pudding","dish/black-pudding"],["Bread sauce","dish/bread-sauce"],["Carpetbag steak","dish/carpetbag-steak"],["Chelsea bun","dish/chelsea-bun"],["Christmas pudding","dish/christmas-pudding"],["Cobbler (food)","dish/cobbler-food"],["Crumpet","dish/crumpet"],["Curry in the United Kingdom","dish/curry-in-the-united-kingdom"],["Digestive biscuit","dish/digestive-biscuit"],["Dundee cake","dish/dundee-cake"],["English muffin","dish/english-muffin"],["Flies' graveyard","dish/flies-graveyard"],["Flummery","dish/flummery"],["Fritter","dish/fritter"],["Garibaldi biscuit","dish/garibaldi-biscuit"],["Hot cross bun","dish/hot-cross-bun"],["Hovis biscuit","dish/hovis-biscuit"],["Jammie Dodgers","dish/jammie-dodgers"],["Melba toast","dish/melba-toast"],["Mince pie","dish/mince-pie"],["Phall","dish/phall"],["Pickled egg","dish/pickled-egg"],["Pie","dish/pie"],["Ploughman's lunch","dish/ploughman-s-lunch"],["Potted shrimps","dish/potted-shrimps"],["Prawn cocktail, steak and Black Forest gateau","dish/prawn-cocktail-steak-and-black-forest-gateau"],["Rich tea","dish/rich-tea"],["Sausage roll","dish/sausage-roll"],["Scotch egg","dish/scotch-egg"],["Shepherd's pie","dish/shepherd-s-pie"],["Soldiers (food)","dish/soldiers-food"],["Spotted dick","dish/spotted-dick"],["Tea sandwich","dish/tea-sandwich"]]}],"img":{"src":"/world-cuisine/img/thumb/1/14/Crumble.jpg/500px-Crumble.jpg","w":500,"h":334,"by":"Unknown author","lic":"Public domain","licUrl":"","page":"https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Crumble.jpg"}},"ingredient/thai-eggplant":{"t":"ingredient","r":"ingredient/thai-eggplant","n":"Thai eggplant","c":"#c9b8dd","crumb":[["Ingredients","ingredients"]],"sub":"Ingredient in 1 dish across 1 cuisine","intro":[["Solanum melongena is a plant species in the nightshade family Solanaceae, grown worldwide for its edible fruit, typically used as a vegetable in cooking. It is known by a number of names, including eggplant, aubergine, and brinjal."]],"src":"wikipedia","actions":[{"kind":"globe","hash":"ingredient-thai-eggplant"},{"kind":"wiki","url":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Thai_eggplant"}],"sections":[{"h":"Where it's cooked with","count":1,"groups":[["Thai cuisine","cuisine/thai-cuisine",[["Green curry","dish/green-curry"]]]]},{"h":"Often cooked with","links":[["Coconut milk","ingredient/coconut-milk"],["Curry","ingredient/curry"],["Fish sauce","ingredient/fish-sauce"],["Kaffir lime","ingredient/kaffir-lime"],["Palm sugar","ingredient/palm-sugar"],["Thai basil","ingredient/thai-basil"]]}],"img":{"src":"/world-cuisine/img/thumb/7/76/Solanum_melongena_24_08_2012_%281%29.JPG/500px-Solanum_melongena_24_08_2012_%281%29.JPG","w":500,"h":546,"by":"Joydeep","lic":"CC BY-SA 3.0","licUrl":"https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by-sa/3.0","page":"https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Solanum_melongena_24_08_2012_(1).JPG"}},"dish/kadyos-baboy-kag-langka":{"t":"dish","r":"dish/kadyos-baboy-kag-langka","n":"Kadyos, baboy, kag langka","c":"#ede6da","crumb":[["Dishes","dishes"]],"sub":"Dish found in 3 cuisines","dot":"#f0b340","intro":[["Kadyos, baboy, kag langka, commonly shortened to KBL, is a Filipino pork soup or stew originating from the Hiligaynon people of the Western Visayas islands. The name of the dish means \"pigeon peas, pork, and jackfruit\" which are the three main ingredients of the soup. The soup is also traditionally soured with batuan fruits (Garcinia binucao). Other souring agents like tamarind can also be used. Other ingredients include leafy greens (like young sweet potato leaves, cabbage, or bokchoi), lemongrass, fish sauce, onions, and siling haba peppers. The pork cut used is typically the hock (pata). The dish is characteristically purple in color due to the use of pigeon peas. It is similar to another Hiligaynon dish known as kadyos, manok, kag ubad which uses chicken and banana pith instead."]],"src":"wikipedia","actions":[{"kind":"globe","hash":"dish-kadyos-baboy-kag-langka"},{"kind":"wiki","url":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Kadyos%2C_baboy%2C_kag_langka"}],"sections":[{"h":"Where it's from","links":[["Filipino cuisine","cuisine/filipino-cuisine"],["Mindanao cuisine","cuisine/filipino-cuisine-mindanao"],["Visayan cuisine","cuisine/filipino-cuisine-visayas"]]},{"h":"In